  "text": "318305 03/18/2026\n08:55 AM B: 1083 P:\n991 RESOLUTN\nPage\n100 of\n119 R $0.00 D $0.00 T $0.00\nClear Creek\nRAAЛОТАОАУСЕНЕНА BII\n1.\nB.\nMETHODS:\nThe fabric should not extend more than 3' above the ground. Cut filter fabric from a\ncontinuous roll to avoid having joints. Where joints are necessary, splice the fabric\nonly at a post, with at least a 6\" overlap, and fasten both ends securely to the post.\nWETD OR OTFEL DOT\nWRE MEAH\nC.\nFasten mesh to uphill side of posts\nFiLTER FAORUO\nwith staples or wire, extend mesh to bottom\noftrench. (Do not\nattach mesh or fabric to trees.)\n3/MA\nD. Dig a 4\"x 4\" trench on the uphill side of the fence.\nE.\nBackfill trench and compact the soil.\nNote: Silt fences will not withstand high-volume of water, snow loading and high wind\nenvironments and often require higher maintenance.\nThe following method should be used to install an erosion control log:\nA. Erosion control logs should conform to the slope and must\nmaintain contact with the ground the entire length of the log. When\ninstalled in series as a wattle, ends should be tightly fastened\ntogether with plastic ties.\nB.\nInstall using 1 ½\" x 1 ½\" x min. 24\" wood stakes.\nLocate stakes at all ends or joints, otherwise on 24\"\ncenters. Alternate orientation throughout the length\nof the erosion control log. Proper staking is essential for erosion control\nlog function.\n100",
